mbined with OpenMP? But that's beyond scope of role C which is about work sharing etc. The goal specifically mentions \"work sharing, reductions, scheduling and thread granularity\". So we should focus on those aspects.\n\nLet's propose: Use dynamic scheduling with chunk size 32 to balance load while keeping overhead moderate. Merge both loops into a single parallel region per iteration using nowait on the first workshare? But previous attempt with nowait failed correctness (5f0dcf1465ab4a51). So we should avoid nowait and instead use two separate #pragma omp for directives within same parallel region, which will have implicit barriers. That is what 09011b12d9b14eb6 did? It said \"merged both loops into a single parallel region per iteration\" but didn't mention nowait; it likely used two workshares with default barrier between them. That was correct.\n\nSo we can do:\n#pragma omp parallel for schedule(dynamic,32) firstprivate(...)\nfor i...\n  compute forces\n// implicit barrier\n#pragma omp parallel for schedule(static) // or same?\nfor i...\n  update positions\n\nBut that would create two parallel regions per iteration (two #pragma omp parallel for). To merge into one region, we need to use a single #pragma omp parallel and then inside have workshares. That reduces thread creation overhead.\n\nLet's write code:\n#pragma omp parallel\n{\n  for (int iter = 0; iter < N_ITER; ++iter) {\n    #pragma omp for schedule(dynamic,32)\n    for i...\n      compute forces\n    // implicit barrier at end of workshare\n    #pragma omp for schedule(static) nowait? Actually we can use same or different.\n    for i...\n      update positions\n  }\n}\nBut careful: the outer loop over iterations is inside parallel region, so each thread executes all iterations. The workshares distribute work among threads within each iteration. This merges loops per iteration and also avoids recreating teams.\n\nHowever, we must ensure that variables like fx,fy,fz are private to each i iteration; the